/-!
# A factor of a principal ideal generated by a nonzerodivisor is invertible

Proof of `Ideal.invertible_of_mul_eq_span_singleton` (Stacks 09ME): if `I` and `J` are ideals of
a commutative ring `A` whose product is the ideal generated by a nonzerodivisor `f ∈ A`, then `I`
is an invertible `A`-module, that is, finite locally free of rank `1`; in particular `I` is a
finitely generated ideal.

The nonzerodivisor hypothesis is used only through the fact that `f` becomes a unit in the total
ring of fractions of `A`, so the mathematics is carried by
`invertible_of_mul_eq_span_singleton_of_isUnit_algebraMap`, which replaces it by that consequence:
`I * J = (f)` and `f` a unit in some faithful commutative `A`-algebra `B`. Write `I·B` for the
image of `I` in the monoid of `A`-submodules of `B`. Taking images along an algebra map is
multiplicative, so `(I·B)(J·B) = (IJ)·B = A·f`, the `A`-span of a unit of `B`, which is a unit of
that monoid; and a factor of a unit in a commutative monoid is a unit, so `I·B` is an invertible
`A`-submodule of `B`. Such a submodule is an invertible `A`-module (`Submodule.tensorInvEquiv`),
and `I ≃ₗ[A] I·B` because `A → B` is injective.

Stacks 09ME is that lemma at `B = FractionRing A`, which for a commutative ring `A` is the
localization at the multiplicative set of all nonzerodivisors: the hypothesis
`f ∈ nonZeroDivisors A` says exactly that `f` is inverted there, and the injectivity of
`A → FractionRing A`, which is that same fact for every element of the set, is already an
instance.

## Main results

* `Stacks.IdealTheory.InvertibleFactor.solution`: the statement of Tag 09ME.

## Implementation notes

The Stacks project argues by a partition of unity: writing `f = ∑ xᵢ yᵢ` with `xᵢ ∈ I` and `yᵢ ∈ J`,
and `xᵢ yᵢ = aᵢ f`, cancellation of the nonzerodivisor `f` in `f = (∑ aᵢ) f` gives `∑ aᵢ = 1`, so
the standard opens `D(aᵢ)` cover `Spec(A)`; over `A_{aᵢ}` one has `f = x y` with `x` and `y`
nonzerodivisors and `I = (x)`, so `I` is free of rank `1` on that cover, which is finite local
freeness of rank `1` (Stacks 00NX).

That partition of unity is exactly the one inside `Submodule.tensorInvEquiv`, Mathlib's proof
(Lemma 2.2 in [RobertsSingh1993]) that a unit `I` of the monoid of submodules of a faithful
algebra satisfies `I ⊗[A] I⁻¹ ≃ₗ[A] A`: there `1 = ∑ aᵢ bᵢ` with `aᵢ ∈ I` and `bᵢ ∈ I⁻¹` is what
splits `I` off a finite free module. The route above therefore reproduces the source's argument
with its conclusion in the equivalent global form — bijectivity of `Iᵛ ⊗[A] I → A` — instead of
freeness on a cover of `Spec(A)`. The only work left is to exhibit `I` as such a unit, and the
total ring of fractions is where the inverse `f⁻¹ J` of `I` lives. Stacks 00NX, which the
source invokes to pass from freeness on a cover to the conclusion, is consequently not used:
`Module.Invertible` is that conclusion, and the passage back to a cover is
`Module.Invertible.exists_finset_free_localization`.

The general lemma is stated over a commutative semiring, since no step of it needs additive
inverses, and it takes the faithful algebra `B` as an argument instead of fixing the total ring of
fractions: the choice of `B` is the whole of the specialization back to the tagged statement.

## References

* [StacksProject 09ME](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/09ME)
-/

namespace Stacks.IdealTheory.InvertibleFactor

/-- If two ideals `I` and `J` of a commutative semiring `A` satisfy `I * J = (f)` for an element
`f` whose image in a faithful commutative `A`-algebra `B` is a unit, then `I` is an invertible
`A`-module: the image of `I` in the monoid of `A`-submodules of `B` is a factor of the unit `A·f`,
hence itself a unit, and `I` is isomorphic to it. -/
private theorem invertible_of_mul_eq_span_singleton_of_isUnit_algebraMap {A : Type*}
    [CommSemiring A] (B : Type*) [CommSemiring B] [Algebra A B] [FaithfulSMul A B] {I J : Ideal A}
    {f : A} (hIJ : I * J = Ideal.span {f}) (hf : IsUnit (algebraMap A B f)) :
    Module.Invertible A I := by
  set φ : A →ₐ[A] B := Algebra.ofId A B
  have hIJB : IsUnit (Submodule.map φ.toLinearMap I * Submodule.map φ.toLinearMap J) := by
    rw [← Submodule.map_mul, hIJ, Submodule.map_span, Set.image_singleton]
    exact hf.map (Submodule.spanSingleton A)
  obtain ⟨u, hu⟩ := isUnit_of_mul_isUnit_left hIJB
  exact .congr (LinearEquiv.ofEq _ _ hu ≪≫ₗ (Submodule.equivMapOfInjective φ.toLinearMap
    (FaithfulSMul.algebraMap_injective A B) I).symm)

public theorem solution {A : Type*} [CommRing A] {I J : Ideal A}
    {f : A} (hf : f ∈ nonZeroDivisors A) (hIJ : I * J = Ideal.span {f}) :
    Module.Invertible A I :=
  -- In the total ring of fractions of `A`, the nonzerodivisor `f` is a unit.
  invertible_of_mul_eq_span_singleton_of_isUnit_algebraMap (FractionRing A) hIJ
    (IsLocalization.map_units _ ⟨f, hf⟩)

end Stacks.IdealTheory.InvertibleFactor

/-!
# A factor of a principal ideal generated by a nonzerodivisor is invertible

Let `A` be a commutative ring and let `I` and `J` be ideals of `A` whose product is the principal
ideal generated by a nonzerodivisor `f`, that is, `I * J = (f)`. Then `I` is an invertible
`A`-module, and by symmetry so is `J`. Being invertible, `I` and `J` are finitely generated ideals
and finite locally free of rank `1`. The slogan is that a product of ideals is an invertible
module iff both factors are, the interesting direction being the one proved here: an invertible
product forces each factor to be invertible, even though neither factor need be a unit in the
multiplicative monoid of ideals of `A`.

The author's argument is a partition of unity. Write `f = ∑ xᵢ yᵢ` with `xᵢ ∈ I` and `yᵢ ∈ J`, and
write `xᵢ yᵢ = aᵢ f` with `aᵢ ∈ A`; cancelling the nonzerodivisor `f` in `f = (∑ aᵢ) f` gives
`∑ aᵢ = 1`, so the standard opens `D(aᵢ)` cover `Spec(A)` and it suffices to treat each
localization `A_{aᵢ}` separately. Over such a ring `f = x y` with `x ∈ I` and `y ∈ J`, and both
`x` and `y` are nonzerodivisors; then `I = (x)`, because `x' ∈ I` satisfies `x' y = a f = a x y`
for some `a ∈ A`, whence `x' = a x`. So `I` becomes free of rank `1` on a standard open cover,
which is invertibility (Tag 00NX).

## Main results

* `Ideal.invertible_of_mul_eq_span_singleton`: if `I * J` is the ideal generated by a
  nonzerodivisor, then `I` is an invertible `A`-module.

## Implementation notes

"Finite locally free of rank `1`" is spelled `Module.Invertible A I`, Mathlib's predicate saying
that the canonical evaluation map `Iᵛ ⊗[A] I → A` is bijective. The source's form is recovered
from it and needs no separate conjunct: `Module.Invertible.exists_finset_free_localization` gives
a finite family of elements generating the unit ideal over whose standard opens `I` localizes to a
free module, `Module.Invertible.instLocalizationLocalizedModule` propagates invertibility to each
such localization, and `Module.Invertible.finrank_eq_one` says each of those free modules has rank
`1`. Likewise `I.FG` is `Module.Finite.iff_fg` applied to the instance
`Module.Invertible.instFinite`, so "finitely generated" is a consequence of the conclusion as
stated rather than a second assertion.

Only `I` appears in the conclusion. The hypotheses are symmetric in `I` and `J` up to `mul_comm`,
so the statement applied to the pair `(J, I)` yields `Module.Invertible A J`; a conjunction over
both factors would therefore be an equivalent statement, and Mathlib's own precedent in this
situation is a pair of one-sided lemmas (`Module.Invertible.left` and `Module.Invertible.right`)
rather than a conjunction.

The source's hypotheses `I ≠ 0` and `J ≠ 0` are omitted because they are consequences rather
than hypotheses, so carrying them would make the statement a decorated special case. If `A` is
nontrivial then `f ∈ nonZeroDivisors A` forces `f ≠ 0`, hence `I * J ≠ ⊥`, hence `I ≠ ⊥` and
`J ≠ ⊥` since either being `⊥` annihilates the product. If `A` is trivial then `0` is a
nonzerodivisor and the hypotheses do hold with `I = J = ⊥`, but so does the conclusion: over the
zero ring every module is isomorphic to `A` by `LinearEquiv.ofSubsingleton`, and
`Module.Invertible.congr` transports invertibility of `A` along it.

The statement is kept at ideals of `A` rather than at `R`-submodules of an `R`-algebra `A`.
Mathlib's `Submodule.instInvertible` makes an invertible submodule of a faithful `R`-algebra an
invertible `R`-module, and `I` becomes such a submodule after inverting `f`; the passage uses that
`A → A_f` is injective, which for ideals of `A` is exactly the hypothesis on `f`, whereas for a
general `R`-algebra the faithfulness of `R → (A_f)` is an extra hypothesis rather than a
consequence.

Faithfulness. (1) Both hypotheses are used, and neither is decoration: without
`f ∈ nonZeroDivisors A` the statement fails over `A = ℤ` with `I = ⊥`, `J = ⊤` and `f = 0`, since
the zero module is not invertible over a nontrivial ring, and `I * J = Ideal.span {f}` is what the
conclusion is about; the source's nonvanishing hypotheses are dropped as consequences, as
discussed above. (2) The scalars and the base are the source's: the conclusion is about the
`A`-module `↥I` for the base ring `A` itself, with its `Submodule.module` structure, and no
localization occurs in the statement. (3) The canonical map is pinned, not merely asserted to
exist: `Module.Invertible A I` unfolds to bijectivity of the evaluation map
`contractLeft A ↥I : Iᵛ ⊗[A] I →ₗ[A] A` (a root-namespace declaration), and the resulting
isomorphism is `Module.Invertible.linearEquiv` for that map; the source names no map here, its
assertion being freeness of rank `1` on a cover, which is the same condition. (4) The
instantiation is not degenerate: over `A = Zsqrtd (-5) = ℤ[√-5]` the ideals `I = (2, 1 + √-5)`
and `J = (2, 1 - √-5)` satisfy `I * J = (2)` with `2` a nonzerodivisor, and `I` is not principal,
so the conclusion provides an invertible module that is not free and an ideal that is not a unit
of `Ideal A`; the principal case `I = J = (g)`, `f = g ^ 2` with `g` a nonzerodivisor is a witness
at the other extreme.

## References

* [StacksProject 09ME](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/09ME)

## Tags

invertible module, invertible ideal, finite locally free, rank one, nonzerodivisor, Picard group
-/

namespace Ideal

/-- **A factor of a principal ideal generated by a nonzerodivisor is invertible.** Let `A` be a
commutative ring and let `I` and `J` be ideals of `A` with `I * J = (f)` for a nonzerodivisor
`f ∈ A`. Then `I` is an invertible `A`-module, that is, finite locally free of rank `1`; in
particular `I` is a finitely generated ideal. Applying this to `J * I = (f)` gives the same
conclusions for `J`. -/
@[stacks 09ME "Stated as `Module.Invertible A ↥I` for one factor: the hypotheses are symmetric in
`I` and `J`, and finite generation and local freeness of rank `1` follow from invertibility. The
source's `I ≠ 0` and `J ≠ 0` are consequences of the hypothesis on `f` and are omitted. See
the module docstring."]
public theorem invertible_of_mul_eq_span_singleton {A : Type*} [CommRing A] {I J : Ideal A}
    {f : A} (hf : f ∈ nonZeroDivisors A) (hIJ : I * J = Ideal.span {f}) :
    Module.Invertible A I := by exact Stacks.IdealTheory.InvertibleFactor.solution (f := f) (J := J)
                                  hf hIJ

end Ideal
